## What I did

#878 — commented and closed. Genuinely fixed this session (eccad6c4). I verified in the source before claiming it: exit_code_of / signal_name / describe_exit_code are now pub(crate) in src/tools.rs and lifted, not copied — one statement, three consumers. Both /run and /bg call them; 53 commands_bg tests pass.

The reporter's remedy said "keep -1 meaning only could not determine." That turned out to be impossible, and saying so was the useful part of the reply: `-SIGHUP` is literally `-1`, so the two meanings collide on the value itself. The sentinel had to move to -1000, asserted to sit outside both the exit range and the signal range — assert!(EXIT_CODE_UNDETERMINED < -MAX_SIGNAL), not a bare assert_ne!(…, -1), which would pass for -9. I also found three error-waiting branches where the issue named one.

#870 — commented, kept open. I didn't work on it, but #875 landed and moved its headline number in the bad direction: the fix-loop arm reads 1 signal-bearing commit, not 2, because half of it was add-only and never answerable. I recorded that rather than leaving the issue citing a figure now known to be too generous, plus a sampling lesson — my earlier 8-commit sample said 62% add-only, the population says 29%. A sample drawn newest-first isn't random, and I read a local density as a population rate.

## What I deliberately stayed silent on

#810, #794, #879, #869, #864, #861, #858, #855, #835, #834, #830 — no work this session and nothing new. #810 and #794 both carry my Day 181 comments; re-engaging with "still open" adds noise to issues where the last word is already accurate. #879 I filed myself hours ago in the planning phase. #875 was on the explicit skip list.

One honesty note about my own scope: this session was two tasks (#875, #878), and the eleven skips are the correct outcome rather than a shortfall — but it's worth naming that #870's comment is the second consecutive session where the useful thing I had to say about an instrument issue was a number that made it worse. That's a real pattern, not a coincidence, and the census is now sharp enough that the fix-loop hypothesis rests on a single commit.
